@startuml
title 01 — User Message Acceptance

' ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' What happens when the user sends a message.
' The Orchestrator saves the message, then immediately returns
' 202 Accepted. Planning happens in the background — see Diagram 04.
'
' Key concept — why queue to itself?
'   The Orchestrator is both a REST server (answers HTTP requests) and
'   a background worker (processes planning jobs from the queue).
'   By placing the planning job on the queue before replying, the
'   REST response stays instant. The background worker picks the job
'   up and starts planning without the user waiting.
'
' Safety and input checks (Diagram 10) run as the very first step
' of the background planning flow, NOT here.
' ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

hide footbox
autonumber "<b>[00]"

skinparam responseMessageBelowArrow true
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam wrapWidth 260
skinparam maxMessageSize 260
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam roundcorner 8

skinparam sequence {
  ArrowColor        #2D6A9F
  ArrowFontColor    #2D6A9F
  ArrowFontSize     11

  LifeLineBorderColor         #2D6A9F
  LifeLineBackgroundColor     #EAF3FB

  ParticipantBorderColor      #2D6A9F
  ParticipantBackgroundColor  #DAEAF7
  ParticipantFontColor        #1A3A52
  ParticipantFontSize         12
  ParticipantFontStyle        bold
  ParticipantPadding          8
  ParticipantBorderThickness  1.5

  ActorBorderColor            #1A3A52
  ActorBackgroundColor        #B8D6EE
  ActorFontColor              #1A3A52
  ActorFontSize               12
  ActorFontStyle              bold

  GroupBorderColor            #5A9ABF
  GroupBackgroundColor        #F0F7FC
  GroupHeaderFontColor        #1A3A52
  GroupHeaderFontSize         12
  GroupBorderThickness        1.2

  NoteBackgroundColor         #FFF8DC
  NoteBorderColor             #C8A800
  NoteFontColor               #4A3800
  NoteFontSize                11
}

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam roundcorner 8

' ─── Participants ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
actor       User as "User / UI"
participant Orch as "Orchestrator"
database    DB   as "HANA DB"
queue       Q    as "Agent Queue"

' ─── Flow ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
User -> Orch : POST /orchestrate/async\n(message text, conversation_id?, file_ids[])
activate Orch

Orch -> DB : look up this conversation_id — does it exist?
DB --> Orch : found | not found

alt #FFF4E5 New conversation — no conversation_id provided
  Orch -> DB : create a new conversation record\n+ save the message and any uploaded file references
  DB --> Orch : new conversation_id assigned

else #EAF6EA Existing conversation — conversation_id was provided
  Orch -> DB : append this message to the conversation history\n+ update the "current message" pointer
  DB --> Orch : updated
end

note over Orch, Q
  The Orchestrator now places a "begin planning" job on its own
  internal queue channel (Orchestrator Topic). Its own background
  worker will pick this up and run the full planning flow.

  This is intentional — it means this REST call can return 202
  immediately without making the user wait for AI planning to finish.

  Safety checks (permission + message validation) run at the start
  of planning, not here. See Diagram 06 — Guardrails & Error Handling.
  Planning flow detail is in Diagram 02 — Planning & Task Routing.
end note

Orch -> Q : place "begin planning" job\non the Orchestrator's own queue channel
Q --> Orch : accepted

Orch --> User : 202 Accepted  { conversation_id }
deactivate Orch

@enduml
